cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you discuss Ballarch, please cite it as below.
  Text and diagrams are licensed under CC BY 4.0 (see NOTICE.md); the code
  in this repository has no software license yet.
title: "Ballarch: an architecture of owners, obligations and honest outcomes (Core 2.0 specification, references and benchmark)"
abstract: >-
  Ballarch is an architecture for components that own state and interact
  with systems that can fail, from hardware blocks and firmware to services
  and workflows. Each piece of mutable state has one owner that decides in
  pure functions; a runtime commits decisions atomically and gives every
  outbound request exactly one outcome, Done, NotDone or Unknown, chosen from
  two boundaries in time (the commit and send horizons) and the port's effect
  class. A knowledge algebra over Kleene's three-valued logic bounds what a
  composite operation may claim, so that a composite that replies with its
  settled knowledge can itself be used as a single component, given honest
  adapters, absorbing compensations and a durable call ledger. The
  distribution contains the specification; reference implementations in
  Kotlin, C and SystemVerilog that check the call-protocol rules and the
  knowledge algebra with level-specific mechanisms (composition across
  levels is not tested); a language-neutral conformance-trace checker; an
  Alloy model; and a benchmark of one checkout feature in nine
  implementations (four styles, each naive and careful, and Ballarch), with
  four change requests and a runtime-overhead measurement. In the benchmark,
  the careful versions and Ballarch were equally correct while the
  provider's delivery horizon held. Ballarch moves the classification of
  outcomes, the write-ahead record and crash recovery from each feature into
  its runtime; the feature still writes the reconciliation. This costs code,
  framework types, total complexity and change size.
version: 2.0.0-rc.1
